WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:15.210
I wasn't always very excited about math.

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:21.590
I was more excited about reading novels, and
I thought I would become a writer one day.

00:00:21.590 --> 00:00:27.100
I got excited about it maybe just as a challenge,
but then I realized that it's really nice

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:29.289
and that I enjoy it.

00:00:29.289 --> 00:00:31.970
These were quite difficult times.

00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:34.650
It was during the war.

00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:37.210
Right after the war, I had a lot of opportunities.

00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:40.140
I went to a very good middle school and then
high school.

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:46.080
I think I was the lucky generation because
I was a teenager when things became more stable.

00:00:50.570 --> 00:00:54.520
My main interest is understanding structures
you can put on a surface.

00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:56.310
There are different ways of looking at it.

00:00:56.310 --> 00:01:02.309
Either you have a surface with some additional
geometric structures, or these kinds of problems

00:01:02.309 --> 00:01:06.659
are related to understanding the space of
such structures.

00:01:06.659 --> 00:01:10.880
One very famous example is if you have a billiard
table and you start from from a point and

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:16.479
you hit the ball and it hits the boundaries
and it moves forever, you want to see the

00:01:16.479 --> 00:01:18.179
trajectory of the ball.

00:01:18.179 --> 00:01:21.280
Would it cover all your billiard table?

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:26.810
Can you find closed billiard paths and interestingly
enough, this is an open question in general,

00:01:26.810 --> 00:01:30.229
if you don't put any restrictions on angles
of the polygon that you started.

00:01:30.229 --> 00:01:32.179
There are two types of questions.

00:01:32.179 --> 00:01:36.759
One is: You have a surface with a geometric
structure, and you're trying to understand

00:01:36.759 --> 00:01:39.320
some properties of the geometric structure
that you have.

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:42.899
The other questions are related to: You have
a surface, and you have a geometric structure

00:01:42.899 --> 00:01:45.500
and you start deforming this geometric structure.

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:48.200
And then you want to see what kind of surfaces
you would get.

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:53.929
Some of the problems like, you know the properties
of a generic surface, a random surface.

00:01:53.929 --> 00:01:59.099
But it's really hard to say something about
a single given geometric structure on the

00:01:59.099 --> 00:02:00.099
surface.

00:02:00.099 --> 00:02:05.959
Some of the work that I've done with different
collaborators shows that sometimes a surface

00:02:05.959 --> 00:02:08.440
is very to one of a generic surface.

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:14.640
You can ask these questions about hyperbolic
surfaces or these flat surfaces or different

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:16.380
geometric structures.

00:02:18.170 --> 00:02:24.340
I think these problems are important because
they are related to some other problems.

00:02:24.340 --> 00:02:28.849
Even if you are interested in high-dimensional
manifolds, one way of dealing with them is

00:02:28.849 --> 00:02:32.360
trying to find some nice surface inside of
them.

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:38.349
You end up learning a lot about other spaces
and properties of other action, so it give

00:02:38.349 --> 00:02:39.349
you a lot of information.

00:02:39.349 --> 00:02:43.109
It's not only the question, but the way you
try to solve it.

